[{'["# Elevation_Normalized\\n# Feature description: Normalize the Elevation feature using z-score normalization.\\n# Usefulness: Elevation influences vegetation types and forest cover, but raw elevation may dominate model training due to scale differences with other features. Z-score normalization standardizes elevation, centering it at zero and scaling it to a standard deviation of one, helping balance feature contributions.\\ndf[\'Elevation_Normalized\'] = (df[\'Elevation\'] - df[\'Elevation\'].mean()) / df[\'Elevation\'].std()", "# Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance\\n# Feature description: Calculate the Euclidean distance to hydrology using both Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology.\\n# Usefulness: Vegetation distribution heavily depends on proximity to water sources. Combining horizontal and vertical distances provides a precise measure of the absolute proximity to hydrology features, which are likely important for determining forest cover type.\\ndf[\'Hydrology_Euclidean_Distance\'] = np.sqrt(df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\']2 + df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\']2)", "# Combined_Distance_Sum\\n# Feature description: Sum of Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology, Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways, and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\\n# Usefulness: Aggregating distances to key physical features captures overall spatial proximity, which likely influences forest cover based on accessibility. This feature complements individual distance metrics by highlighting cumulative effects.\\ndf[\'Combined_Distance_Sum\'] = df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] + df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\']", "# Road_Fire_Distance_Ratio\\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways and Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points.\\n# Usefulness: This ratio reflects the spatial relationship between human infrastructure (roadways) and wildfire activity. Areas closer to roadways may have altered fire risk or vegetation composition depending on distance from fire points.\\ndf[\'Road_Fire_Distance_Ratio\'] = df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] / (df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\'] + 1e-6)", "# Hydrology_Road_Distance_Ratio\\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio between Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology and Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways.\\n# Usefulness: Proximity to hydrology relative to roadways may influence forest composition due to accessibility, human interaction, and ecological dynamics. This ratio provides insight into competing spatial influences.\\ndf[\'Hydrology_Road_Distance_Ratio\'] = df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] / (df[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\'] + 1e-6)", "# Soil_Type_Sum\\n# Feature description: Sum all Soil_Type indicators to represent the diversity of soil types at a given location.\\n# Usefulness: Different soil types support various vegetation. Higher soil diversity may indicate richer ecological resources, which could be critical for determining different forest cover types.\\ndf[\'Soil_Type_Sum\'] = df[[f\'Soil_Type{i}\' for i in range(1, 41)]].sum(axis=1)", "# Vertical_Hydrology_Relief\\n# Feature description: Difference between Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology and Slope.\\n# Usefulness: This metric quantifies elevation-hydrology effects adjusted by terrain steepness, capturing drainage or water retention dynamics that influence forest types.\\ndf[\'Vertical_Hydrology_Relief\'] = df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] - df[\'Slope\']", "# Wilderness_Area_Combination\\n# Feature description: Combine Wilderness area indicators into a feature that represents the specific wilderness type (one-hot encoding).\\n# Usefulness: Each wilderness area may have distinct ecological and human management characteristics impacting forest cover. Combining binary indicators into a categorical representation simplifies analysis while preserving domain knowledge.\\ndf[\'Wilderness_Area_Combination\'] = (\\n    df[[\'Wilderness_Area1\', \'Wilderness_Area2\', \'Wilderness_Area3\', \'Wilderness_Area4\']]\\n    .dot([1, 2, 3, 4])\\n)", "# Terrain_Slope_Relief\\n# Feature description: Create a feature combining Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology and Slope using a ratio.\\n# Usefulness: Elevation-driven hydrological effects depend on terrain steepness. This ratio represents hydrological relief adjusted for slope steepness, providing unique insights into geographic variation affecting forest composition.\\ndf[\'Terrain_Slope_Relief\'] = df[\'Vertical_Distance_To_Hydrology\'] / (df[\'Slope\'] + 1e-6)", "# Light_Exposure_Std\\n# Feature description: Calculate the standard deviation of the three Hillshade indices to measure terrain light variability.\\n# Usefulness: Uneven illumination affects microclimates and vegetation growth. High variability may indicate rugged terrain with differing light patterns, which could impact forest cover.\\ndf[\'Light_Exposure_Std\'] = df[[\'Hillshade_9am\', \'Hillshade_Noon\', \'Hillshade_3pm\']].std(axis=1)", "# Combined_Distance_Std\\n# Feature description: Calculate the standard deviation of distances (to hydrology, roadways, and fire points) to assess spatial spread.\\n# Usefulness: This feature quantifies variability in proximity to key physical features. High standard deviation may correlate with diverse ecological dynamics and distinct vegetation types.\\ndf[\'Combined_Distance_Std\'] = df[[\'Horizontal_Distance_To_Hydrology\', \'Horizontal_Distance_To_Roadways\', \'Horizontal_Distance_To_Fire_Points\']].std(axis=1)", "# Combined_Elevation_Ratio\\n# Feature description: Compute the ratio of Elevation to Combined_Distance_Sum.\\n# Usefulness: This feature highlights areas with significant elevation changes relative to spatial proximity to hydrology, roadways, and fire points. Forest composition changes with elevation and geographic characteristics.\\ndf[\'Combined_Elevation_Ratio\'] = df[\'Elevation\'] / (df[\'Combined_Distance_Sum\'] +